{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8fca2f319db4c218","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9017\n3\tepic_count: 2\n4\tcritical_path_length: 2\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\t---\n9\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9017\n10\t\n11\tStrategy: Keyboard accessibility conformance for core Dashboard flows\n12\t\n13\t## Triage Outcome\n14\t\n15\tNot triaged as below-threshold or docs-only. Although the code change itself is\n16\tsmall and well-specified (the audit report enumerates the exact defects per\n17\tflow), the strategy spans two distinct work streams on two components:\n18\t\n19\t1. **Code implementation** (Dashboard): keyboard operability and focus-indicator\n20\t   fixes across the four audited flows.\n21\t2. **Substantive content authoring** (Documentation): the VPAT and the product\n22\t   docs accessibility statement must be updated and republished — new\n23\t   conformance content with technical review, not an incidental touch-up.\n24\t\n25\tPer Step 0 escape condition 2 (code implementation + substantive content\n26\tauthoring are distinct work streams), the full decomposition applies.\n27\t\n28\t## Epic List\n29\t\n30\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n31\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n32\t| RHAISTRAT-9017-E001 | Dashboard keyboard accessibility remediation for audited core flows | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n33\t| RHAISTRAT-9017-E002 | Update and republish accessibility conformance documentation | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Documentation | P2 |\n34\t\n35\t## Dependency DAG\n36\t\n37\t```mermaid\n38\tgraph TD\n39\t    E001[RHAISTRAT-9017-E001 Dashboard keyboard accessibility remediation]\n40\t    E002[RHAISTRAT-9017-E002 Accessibility conformance documentation]\n41\t    E001 --> E002\n42\t```\n43\t\n44\t## DAG Justification\n45\t\n46\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n47\t|------|------|-----------|\n48\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring blocked by all Implementations) | The VPAT and accessibility statement describe the fixed state; they must be authored after the keyboard fixes land and be technically reviewed against the implementation. |\n49\t\n50\tNo other edges: only one code epic exists, so no implementation-to-implementation\n51\tedges apply. There are no external dependencies, no new components requiring an\n52\tonboarding chain, and no open questions (the strategy states the audit report\n53\tenumerates the exact defects per flow), so no Investigation epics are needed.\n54\t\n55\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n56\t\n57\t| HLR | Priority | Epic | Coverage |\n58\t|-----|----------|------|----------|\n59\t| HLR-1 (four core flows operable by keyboard, no focus traps or unreachable controls) | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9017-E001 | Full |\n60\t| HLR-2 (visible, consistent focus indicators across audited flows) | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9017-E001 | Full |\n61\t| HLR-3 (VPAT and accessibility statement updated and republished, with technical review) | P2 | RHAISTRAT-9017-E002 | Full |\n62\t\n63\tAll HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n64\t\n65\t## Design Decisions\n66\t\n67\t- **HLR-1 and HLR-2 bundled into one epic.** Both concern the same logical\n68\t  change (keyboard accessibility remediation of the four audited flows), on the\n69\t  same component, same team, and the same UI surfaces (modals and controls in\n70\t  those flows). Focus-trap fixes and focus-indicator consistency touch the same\n71\t  components; splitting them would make two epics modify the same UI surfaces\n72\t  independently.\n73\t- **E002 priority = P2** via the docs-authoring priority exception: the strategy\n74\t  frontmatter priority is Normal → P2. This matches HLR-3's own P2 priority;\n75\t  the docs epic does not push priority upstream to E001.\n76\t- **Documentation component mapping.** \"Documentation\" is a canonical RHAI\n77\t  component in `.context/rhai-components.txt` but has no architecture-context\n78\t  file. It is a content-process component, not a code component, so the\n79\t  appropriate routing is a `docs-authoring` epic rather than a\n80\t  repo/konflux onboarding chain (Rule 20 does not apply — nothing is being\n81\t  provisioned). The \"RHOAI Documentation\" team assignment is inferred; the\n82\t  strategy provides no Impacted Teams table (see Health Warnings).\n83\t- **Scope level.** Epic scope stays at the functional level of the strategy\n84\t  (keyboard operability, focus retention in modals, indicator consistency).\n85\t  Specific implementation techniques (focus-trap utilities, indicator styling\n86\t  approach) are left to the implementing team.\n87\t\n88\t## Health Warnings\n89\t\n90\t- **Missing strategy sections.** The strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no\n91\t  Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then Acceptance Criteria, and no\n92\t  Non-Functional Requirements section. The decomposition proceeded with\n93\t  available information; team assignments were taken from architecture context\n94\t  (Dashboard → RHOAI Dashboard) and inferred for Documentation.\n95\t- **Scope trap (mild).** HLR-3 requires a *published* conformance statement\n96\t  before customer renewal. Publication channels and review sign-off for a VPAT\n97\t  are a human process gate outside the code work; the docs epic carries an\n98\t  explicit technical-review AC, but the renewal deadline itself is a business\n99\t  commitment, not an engineering AC.\n100\t- **Out-of-scope boundary.** Screen-reader semantics beyond what the keyboard\n101\t  fixes require and flows outside the four audited ones are explicitly out of\n102\t  scope; acceptance criteria below are scoped to the four audited flows only.\n103\t\n104\t## Tiered Delivery\n105\t\n106\tNot applicable — no external dependency with uncertain timing. The single\n107\tdependency (docs after code) is internal to the strategy.\n108\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-828654e62c7f85d6","agent_